A standard clone conflates the object database with a single working
directory: the '.git' directory sits inside the root of your source files.

A bare repository removes this confusion. It contains only objects,
refs, and configuration—no checked-out files.

By placing a bare repository in a hidden directory, the project root
becomes a clean container for peer worktrees:
    project/
        .bare/
        main/
        feature/

In a linked worktree, '.git' is not a directory; it is a one-line ASCII
file pointing to the parent database:
    gitdir: /path/to/project/.bare/worktrees/main
